Policies Structure:- AWS IAM policies are JSON documents made up of top-level elements and one or more Statement blocks.
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "S3-Account-Permissions",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowS3ReadWrite",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:root"
      },
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*",
      "Condition": {
        "Bool": {
          "aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

Top-Level Structure

Version: The IAM policy language version (standard is "2012-10-17").
Id (Optional): An optional identifier for the overall policy document.
Statement (Required): An array containing one or more individual permission statements.
Statement Elements
Sid (Optional): Statement ID used to describe or distinguish individual statements.
Effect (Required): Defines whether the statement grants or denies access ("Allow" or "Deny").
Principal (Required in resource-based policies): Specifies the account, IAM user, role, or federated user allowed or denied access. (Omitted in identity-based policies attached directly to users, groups, or roles).
Action (Required): The specific AWS API actions being permitted or denied (e.g., "s3:GetObject", "ec2:RunInstances").
Resource (Required in identity-based policies): The AWS resources (defined via ARN) to which the actions apply (e.g., "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*").
Condition (Optional): Key-value rules that dictate when the policy is in effect (e.g., IP address restrictions, requiring MFA, or date ranges).